$DIR/main.p:30:8: b is moved into take2 (its parameter takes a Buf by value) here, but a view of it (&b, line 29) was stored in h at line 29, where the compiler cannot check its uses
    A view of an owner held in a local variable may be used only while the
    variable keeps the value the view was made from. The compiler follows views
    through local variables and their fields, and into the arguments of the
    calls that store them there, but the view of b made by &b at line 29 went
    into memory it does not follow, so it cannot check that the view is not used
    after line 30.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Keep the view in a local variable or a field of one, where the compiler
    follows it, or do not move it while the view is kept
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3314" for a longer explanation)
